    "notes": "Recorded from Claude Code 2.1.228 driving a disposable git repository under TMPDIR, never the AutoStack checkout. Machine-specific values are normalized to TRANSCRIPT_PLACEHOLDERS in both the frames and the recorded argv: the temp repository to /tmp/agent-workspace, the home directory to /home/agent, the username to agent. The `system/init` frame carries the operator's global hooks, plugins, skills, agents, slash commands and connected MCP servers, which are machine shape rather than protocol shape; those six keys are replaced by the values observed on the clean `--setting-sources project` re-run of the same session, and every other init field (session_id, uuid, cwd, model, permissionMode, claude_code_version) is the recording's own. `result` is a TURN boundary, not a session boundary: a steered session emits one `result` per turn, so `completed` must be minted on session end. PERMISSION CHANNEL: `--permission-mode manual` is NOT a round trip -- it auto-denies and continues, emitting `system/permission_denied` with nothing on stdin able to answer it. The hidden-but-accepted `--permission-prompt-tool` flag is the real channel: Claude Code spawns the configured MCP stdio server itself and calls its tool before executing the gated tool, and the returned decision genuinely gates the side effect (the probe replied {\"behavior\":\"deny\"} and notes.txt was never created). `_meta[\"claudecode/toolUseId\"]` correlates the ask to the assistant frame's tool_use block. ORDERING CAVEAT -- READ BEFORE WRITING THE MAPPER: the approval request is emitted here BEFORE the assistant tool_use frame because D-3 requires that ordering of the normalized stream, but that is NOT the order the two real channels produced. In the live capture the assistant `tool_use` frame reached Claude Code's stdout at 2026-08-27T23:18:39.326Z and the MCP `tools/call approve` request reached the permission server at 23:18:39.335Z -- the CLI announces the tool call about nine milliseconds BEFORE it asks. They are two independent channels with no ordering guarantee between them, so D-3 compliance cannot come from provider ordering; the adapter MUST buffer a gated `tool_use` block and emit no `tool_call` of any phase until the decision lands. Task 8 owes a dedicated test for the observed stdout-first interleaving, which this fixture deliberately does not reproduce. DECISION CAVEAT: the recorded decision was a denial (the probe denies by design so nothing was ever written), so the frames after `awaitStdin` are the denial path -- the tool_result carries is_error true with non_execution_kind \"permission-rule\", and the terminal `result` carries a populated `permission_denials` array. No allow-path Claude capture exists yet. Frame order records the MEASURED provider behaviour (2026-08-27): Claude Code announces the tool_use on stdout ~10ms BEFORE it asks over the MCP permission socket, on a separate channel with no ordering guarantee. Per plan decision D-3 the fixture must not stage an approval-first ordering the provider does not have -- the adapter earns the no-side-effect-before-decision rule by buffering on tool_use.id."
  },
